WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.218
THOMAS FRANTZEN: Roar.

00:00:01.218 --> 00:00:02.009
STEPHANIE WONG: Ah!

00:00:02.009 --> 00:00:03.260
Dinosaur!

00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:03.900
Or, wait.

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:05.130
Tom, is that you?

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:07.555
And weren't you in
Tokyo just last week?

00:00:07.555 --> 00:00:08.070
THOMAS FRANTZEN:
Yes, which is why

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:09.736
I'm here to share my
excitement of Japan

00:00:09.736 --> 00:00:11.130
and its classic movies.

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:11.520
STEPHANIE WONG: Awesome.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:12.603
Can't wait for the sequel.

00:00:12.603 --> 00:00:14.370
Maybe you'll be the
dinosaur and I'll

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:15.777
be the scientist, Dr. Wong.

00:00:15.777 --> 00:00:17.610
Now, what are you going
to be demoing today?

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:18.190
THOMAS FRANTZEN:
Well, today I'm going

00:00:18.190 --> 00:00:20.970
to be demoing a custom machine
learning API that leverages

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:25.140
Google's ML APIs, Vision,
Speech, Translation and Natural

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:26.104
Language.

00:00:26.104 --> 00:00:26.770
But don't worry.

00:00:26.770 --> 00:00:29.550
It's not going to turn into a
crazy chemical monster like me.

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:31.990
It's going to be an easily
integratable solution.

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:32.100
STEPHANIE WONG: Sweet.

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:33.141
Let's jump right into it.

00:00:33.141 --> 00:00:36.024
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:38.945 --> 00:00:40.070
THOMAS FRANTZEN: All right.

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:42.000
So what you're seeing
here on the left side

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.175
is a video stream.

00:00:43.175 --> 00:00:44.800
This is actually
using something called

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:46.980
WebRTC, which is a
real-time communication

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:49.740
API released by Google.

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:53.510
And it pretty much allows you to
use the webcam in your computer

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:55.480
and in your web browser.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:57.240
So we use those feeds
to actually drive

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:58.110
four different APIs.

00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:00.730
And the first one that you
actually see is the Vision API.

00:01:00.730 --> 00:01:02.180
So two features that come out--

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:05.910
that come out of the Vision
API are faces and labels.

00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:08.370
Now, with faces what
we're actually using

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:10.920
is the-- we're trying to figure
out the emotional state out

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:12.180
of your face.

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:17.460
So if we smile, you're
going to see that it says,

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:19.470
joy is very likely.

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:21.617
So it provides cool
insight into kind

00:01:21.617 --> 00:01:23.200
of your emotional
state of individuals

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:26.450
that you find within an image.

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:28.030
Another cool feature is Labels.

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:31.180
So Labels pretty much
identify different things that

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:32.880
are found within the image.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:34.460
So in our case,
you know, there's

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:40.437
a forehead, or a socialite,
different-- a smile.

00:01:40.437 --> 00:01:42.770
STEPHANIE WONG: I've finally
become Kim Kardashian, yes.

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:45.604
THOMAS FRANTZEN:
That's exactly right.

00:01:45.604 --> 00:01:48.020
So you can use these to help
identify different things you

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:50.600
may find within an image.

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:52.747
So moving on to
the next API, which

00:01:52.747 --> 00:01:54.580
is the Speech API, what
we actually did here

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:57.170
is we're using the
streaming Speech API.

00:01:57.170 --> 00:02:00.950
And we took the audio stream
from the WebRTC video feed

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:05.240
and fed it through a websocket
to our compute engine

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:06.560
incense in the back end.

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:09.470
And with that, what we
can do is transcribe text

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:10.490
in relative real time.

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:19.270
So what that does is kind--
it pretty much opens up

00:02:19.270 --> 00:02:22.820
the door to the other APIs that
are available through Google.

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:25.840
So for example, maybe you
want to do translation.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:28.060
Maybe you're
speaking to somebody

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:29.480
in a different language.

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:31.110
So I was just in Japan.

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:34.450
What if you needed to
talk somebody in Japanese?

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:37.940
So with a Speech
API and Translate,

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:41.709
you're able to do this
in relative real time.

00:02:41.709 --> 00:02:42.500
Pretty cool, right?

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:42.720
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:44.790
So once you get
into Text, then you

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:47.312
can use that as input
for Translation and NLP.

00:02:47.312 --> 00:02:48.270
THOMAS FRANTZEN: Right.

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:49.140
Exactly.

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:52.200
Now NLP, Natural Language,
as I said, identifies

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:53.977
different objects
within your statement

00:02:53.977 --> 00:02:55.560
and figures out what
kind of sentiment

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:57.000
do you have around that.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:02.010
So like let's say, for
instance, I'm Godzilla.

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:05.356
I love Google Cloud Platform.

00:03:05.356 --> 00:03:06.980
You'll see that down
on the bottom here

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:09.140
there's a whole bunch
of different objects

00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:12.110
that it found important within
that statement, the importance

00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:14.270
of those objects,
as well as kind

00:03:14.270 --> 00:03:16.130
how you're feeling about it.

00:03:16.130 --> 00:03:17.130
STEPHANIE WONG: Awesome.

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:20.760
Now we can tell just how
much people love Godzilla

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:21.870
and Google Cloud Platform.

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:23.380
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:25.230
Wow, Tom, for a
terrorizing dinosaur,

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:26.530
that was pretty awesome.

00:03:26.530 --> 00:03:26.730
THOMAS FRANTZEN: Yeah.

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:28.016
I'm looking forward to
creating more concoctions

00:03:28.016 --> 00:03:29.031
and demos in the future.

00:03:29.031 --> 00:03:30.030
STEPHANIE WONG: Awesome.

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:30.780
I'm not sure if
you're going to be

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:32.340
able to do that with
your short, stubby arms.

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:33.720
But I guess we'll find out.

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:35.860
THOMAS FRANTZEN: Please comment,
like, and subscribe below.

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:37.390
STEPHANIE WONG: And if you
want to get in touch with us,

00:03:37.390 --> 00:03:39.223
check out the link in
the description below.

00:03:39.223 --> 00:03:41.870
[MUSIC PLAYING]

